# Reproduction and interpretation

Run `python3 analysis/estimate_pi.py` from the repository root. Only Python and NumPy are needed; measured versions and code digest are recorded in results/provenance.json. This regenerates all CSV/JSON results. No observed inputs are supplied: the input is the frozen objective in experiment-repo issue #1, quoted in brief.md.

The seed and point order are fixed: for each N, reset default_rng(2026+i) for i=0,...,49 and draw shape (N,2). Each row is (x,y); count x*x+y*y <= 1. Therefore larger N shares the initial points for a given run index. Runs within each N are independent pseudorandom streams; estimates across N are paired. The requested unweighted fit uses the three natural-log mean absolute errors and includes an intercept. Absolute errors are averaged, rather than taking the absolute error of an average pi estimate.

Theoretical context follows directly from K~Binomial(N,p), p=pi/4. The estimator 4K/N is unbiased with variance pi(4-pi)/N. Its normal approximation gives E|error| approximately sqrt(2/pi)*sqrt(pi*(4-pi)/N), hence exponent -1/2. The normal-approximation column is a reference, not a measured value or exact finite-N mean absolute error.

The optional uncertainty diagnostic resamples all 50 run-index triplets together 10,000 times using separate seed 92026 and refits the log-MAE slope. Its percentile interval describes finite-run uncertainty approximately and is not an OLS interval assuming independent residuals at the three N values. Adjacent slopes show sensitivity to which decade is compared. These diagnostics support cautious interpretation; the requested report needs only the three MAEs, main exponent and comparison to -1/2.
